Preface Exercises

Summary of Codes
M: Mathematically-oriented
HM: Requires "higher math"

00: Immediate
10: Simple (60s)
20: Medium (15m)
30: Moderately Hard
40: Term Project
50: Research Problem

Exercises

1. [00] What does rating "M20" mean?

Got it in one, Dr. Knuth. An M20 problem is mathematically-oriented and can be solved in about 15 minutes.

2. [10] Of what value can the exercises in a textbook be to the reader?

15s: Exercises allow you to synthesize both theory and pratice.
60s: Theory will only get you so far.

3. [14] Prove that 13^3 = 2197. Generalize your answer. [This is a horrible problem we try to avoid in this book...]

60s or so

13*13*13 = 13*169 = 2197. What does he mean by generalize your answer? Write a program that can compute x^3 for any x? OK...

function cubed(x) { return x*x*x; }

4. [HM45] Prove that when n is an integer, n > 2, the equation x^n + y^n + z^n has no solution in positive integers x, y, z.

lol - Fermat's Last Theorem - ChatGPT claims it was solved by Andrew Wiles in 1994.



Procedure for Reading TAOCP
---------------------------
Preface pages xii through xiv introduce the reader to Knuth's humor *and* algorithmic sense. Nice.

In case you missed it:
* you'll be reading TAOCP forever
* you get to sleep when you're tired
* you need to enlist the help of your friends


Ratings
-------

Unlike Dr. Bellman, Knuth has decided to rank his problems beforehand. How magnanimous!

I've paraphrased them below.

00 Very Easy -- So easy your brain can do it
10 Simple -- So easy ChatGPT can one-shot it
20 Average -- You can probably solve this with effort
30 Moderate -- 2+ hours of work
40 Difficult -- days worth of work
50 Bellman -- An open research problemApril 23, 2025

Euclid's algorithm and numbered lists.April 24, 2025

